    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `build_server()` in `app/server.py`, and both tools work when called over stdio from a real MCP client.\n\n**How it's built:** mcp 2.2.0 renamed `FastMCP` to `mcp.server.mcpserver.MCPServer`, so code that imports `mcp.server.fastmcp` now fails straight away. `build_server()` creates `MCPServer(\"notes\")` and registers `add_note` and `word_count` with `@server.tool()`. `python -m app.server` still serves over stdio.\n\n**Error for an unknown title:** `word_count` raises `ToolError(f\"no such note: {title}\")`. In mcp 2.x this is the only kind of exception whose message reaches the model. Any other exception, such as `KeyError` or `ValueError`, reaches the model only as \"Error executing tool word_count\", so it wouldn't contain \"no such note\".\n\n**Checks:**\n- `pytest -q`: 1 passed. The only existing test checks that `NOTES` starts empty; there are no tests for the tools themselves.\n- `python -m app.smoke` lists `add_note` and `word_count`.\n- An end-to-end run through a real `ClientSession` gave:\n  - `add_note(\"a\", \"one two  three\\nfour\")` \u2192 `\"saved a\"`\n  - `word_count(\"a\")` \u2192 `4`\n  - `word_count(\"zz\")` \u2192 `is_error=True`, with the text `Error executing tool word_count: no such note: zz`\n\n**Leftover file:** `_e2e_check.py` in the project root is the throwaway script I used for that last check. The command to delete it was held for approval, so it's still there and you can delete it yourself. No dependencies were added or changed."
  },
